    "# <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"44\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:10px;\"> Theory Foundation: Layers, Loss, Backpropagation, and Representation Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "A neuron computes a weighted sum followed by a nonlinear activation:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z=w^Tx+b,\\qquad a=\\phi(z).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Stacking layers gives $a^{(\\ell)}=\\phi(W^{(\\ell)}a^{(\\ell-1)}+b^{(\\ell)})$. Without nonlinear activations, several linear layers collapse into one linear transformation, so depth would not add representational power. ReLU, $\\phi(z)=\\max(0,z)$, is common because it is simple and has a useful gradient for positive inputs.\n",
    "\n",
    "For binary classification, the output can be a sigmoid probability and the loss can be binary cross-entropy,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L(\\theta)=-\\frac1n\\sum_i\\left[y_i\\log \\hat p_i+(1-y_i)\\log(1-\\hat p_i)\\right].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Training updates parameters by gradient descent or a variant such as Adam:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\eta\\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta_t).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Backpropagation is an efficient application of the chain rule. If $L$ depends on $a$, $a$ on $z$, and $z$ on $w$, then $\\partial L/\\partial w=(\\partial L/\\partial a)(\\partial a/\\partial z)(\\partial z/\\partial w)$. It attributes the final error backward through every operation; it is not a separate learning objective.\n",
    "\n",
    "Neural networks learn representations because hidden activations become features optimized for the target. More parameters can model complicated interactions, but also noise and proxies. Early stopping, weight decay, dropout, data augmentation, and architecture limits are forms of regularization. Training loss measures fit to optimization rows; validation loss guides choices; the test set estimates the already-fixed procedure.\n",
    "\n",
    "Optimization can fail through poor scaling, unsuitable learning rates, saturation, or unstable gradients. A converged loss does not establish that the target is meaningful, probabilities are calibrated, or group errors are acceptable.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Questions you should be ready to answer\n",
    "\n",
    "- Why does a network need nonlinear activations?\n",
    "- What does backpropagation compute, and how is that different from gradient descent?\n",
    "- Why can training loss improve while validation loss gets worse?\n",
    "- What is learned by a hidden layer, and why need it not match a human concept?"
   ]
  },
