    "# Part 2: Prepare the Inputs Without Leakage\n",
    "\n",
    "Neural networks are sensitive to numeric scale. The preprocessing must be learned from training observations and reused unchanged for validation and test observations.\n",
    "\n",
    "This tutorial treats object/string fields as categorical and other non-ID predictors as numeric. It uses one-hot encoding, imputation, and standardization inside one pipeline.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t9-01",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Input Representation Shapes Every Later Layer\n",
    "\n",
    "The network receives numeric coordinates, not original meanings. One-hot encoding represents categories, imputation fills missing inputs, and standardization places numeric measurements on comparable scales. These transformations must be learned from the training rows so evaluation information does not influence the fitted representation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "The first layer only sees the numeric representation produced by scaling and encoding. If $x'=D^{-1}(x-\\mu)$, fitting $\\mu$ or $D$ on validation rows leaks information. One-hot encoding creates separate coordinates for categories; embeddings instead learn dense coordinates. Neither representation makes the underlying category definition neutral or complete.\n"
   ]
  },
