   "source": [
    "# Part 3: Fit a Multilayer Feedforward Network\n",
    "\n",
    "The required model has two hidden layers with 64 and 32 units. ReLU supplies nonlinearity. The Adam optimizer uses stochastic gradient information to update weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "Early stopping holds out part of the training data as an internal validation set. Training stops when validation performance does not improve for several epochs.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a small dense neural network. It demonstrates deep-learning ideas without claiming the scale or architecture of a modern language or vision model.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t9-02",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Optimization Updates Weights Rather Than Solving Once\n",
    "\n",
    "Forward propagation produces predictions and loss. Backpropagation calculates how the loss changes with each weight, and Adam uses that gradient information to update the network repeatedly. `max_iter` is a safety limit on training iterations, not a required number the model must use; convergence or early stopping can end training sooner.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "One epoch processes the training data once, usually in mini-batches. A learning rate that is too large can jump across useful regions; one that is too small can make progress impractically slow. Adam adapts coordinate-wise step sizes using moving averages of gradients, but it does not eliminate tuning. Random initialization means repeated fits can differ, so seeds and stability checks matter.\n"
   ]
  },
