   "source": [
    "# Part 4: Inspect Learning Curves\n",
    "\n",
    "Training loss should generally decrease. The internal validation score helps reveal whether additional epochs continue improving held-out training performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Early stopping is a regularization tool, not a guarantee against overfitting.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t9-03",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Training and Validation Curves Answer Different Questions\n",
    "\n",
    "Training loss shows how well the network is fitting the rows used for updates. Validation performance estimates whether those updates continue to help on held-out training rows. A widening gap can suggest overfitting, while weak performance on both can suggest underfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "High training and validation loss suggest underfitting or failed optimization. Falling training loss with rising validation loss suggests overfitting. A persistent gap can reflect model variance, distribution mismatch, or leakage-free difficulty. Early stopping selects an epoch using validation data, so that validation subset is part of model selection and must not be reused as the final test.\n"
   ]
  },
