   "source": [
    "# AI for Social Good: More Flexible Models Require Stronger Human-Centered Checks\n",
    "\n",
    "The earlier course’s responsible-AI discussions emphasized that AI can improve healthcare, education, public services, and other domains while also amplifying bias, enabling surveillance, or silencing marginalized voices. Deep learning increases this tension because a flexible model can learn useful patterns that are difficult to inspect directly.\n",
    "\n",
    "For any high-impact use, responsible analysis should include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- independent validation across populations, locations, and time;\n",
    "- subgroup error and calibration analysis, including the worst-performing groups;\n",
    "- a clearly stated intended use and prohibited uses;\n",
    "- privacy protection and minimal data collection;\n",
    "- understandable notice that an automated system is involved;\n",
    "- explanations appropriate to the people affected, not only to developers;\n",
    "- human review, appeal, and a non-automated fallback when feasible;\n",
    "- monitoring after deployment for distribution shift and unintended feedback.\n",
    "\n",
    "Human-centered design means involving the people who experience the system when deciding which errors, explanations, and fallback options are acceptable.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Social-good principle:** Model flexibility increases what the system can learn—and increases the burden to prove that its behavior remains safe, fair, understandable, and contestable in the real world.\n"
   ]
  },
