   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: More Layers Mean More Capacity, Not Automatic Improvement\n",
    "\n",
    "Additional units and layers add parameters and allow more complicated representations. They also create more ways to fit noise. Architecture is therefore a tuning choice evaluated within training data, not a ladder where deeper always means better.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "If layer widths are $d_0,d_1,\\ldots,d_L$, the number of weights is roughly $\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{L}d_{\\ell-1}d_\\ell$ plus biases. Capacity can grow very quickly. Depth can represent some compositional functions efficiently, but optimization, data size, regularization, and the target determine whether that capacity helps. Architecture comparisons should report variability, not only the largest observed score.\n"
   ]
  },
