    "# Part 9: From Neural Networks to Transformers and LLMs\n",
    "\n",
    "An LLM is still a neural network trained by gradient-based optimization. The major change is the **transformer** architecture: instead of processing each word with a fixed neighborhood or recurrent memory, it lets each token compute which earlier tokens are most relevant.\n",
    "\n",
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: A Transformer Is a Learned Information-Routing System\n",
    "\n",
    "Text is first split into **tokens**. Each token ID is mapped to an embedding vector, and position information is added so the model can distinguish “dog bites person” from “person bites dog.” For one attention head, every token produces a query $Q$, key $K$, and value $V$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Q=XW_Q,\\qquad K=XW_K,\\qquad V=XW_V.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Scaled dot-product attention is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)\n",
    "=\\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}+M\\right)V.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The query–key dot products are relevance scores. Softmax turns each row into nonnegative weights that sum to one. Multiplying by $V$ creates a weighted mixture of information from relevant token positions. The factor $\\sqrt{d_k}$ prevents dot products from growing too large as dimension increases. In a causal language model, mask $M$ blocks future tokens, so the model cannot look ahead during next-token prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Multi-head attention** repeats this calculation with several learned projections, allowing different heads to represent different relationships. Each transformer block also contains a small feedforward network applied to every position, residual connections that preserve an information path, and normalization that stabilizes training.\n",
    "\n",
    "### How this becomes an LLM\n",
    "\n",
    "During pretraining, a causal LLM repeatedly solves\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\min_\\theta -\\sum_{t}\\log P_\\theta(x_t\\mid x_{<t}),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "meaning: assign high probability to the actual next token given earlier tokens. Across enormous text collections, the weights learn statistical representations useful for grammar, facts, style, and many task patterns. Generation repeats the same operation one token at a time. The model does not retrieve a guaranteed true sentence; it samples or selects from a learned conditional distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "### A simple metaphor—and its limit\n",
    "\n",
    "Imagine every word holding a question card: “Which earlier words help me update my meaning?” Attention assigns weights to possible helpers and blends their information. This metaphor explains routing, but it should not be mistaken for human attention, understanding, or verified reasoning.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What you should be able to explain\n",
    "\n",
    "- embeddings turn token IDs into learnable vectors;\n",
    "- position information preserves order;\n",
    "- self-attention mixes information across tokens;\n",
    "- the causal mask prevents looking at future tokens;\n",
    "- pretraining learns next-token probabilities;\n",
    "- scale and flexibility increase both capability and the need for evaluation."
   ]
  },
